WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.888 --> 00:00:03.304
[BLING SOUND]

00:00:08.510 --> 00:00:12.210
And so I'm really
excited to be here today

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:15.660
to have this opportunity
to talk with you

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:18.340
and be invited to give a talk.

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:20.500
And so first, I
want to say, I get

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:22.397
to be the one here
to give a talk.

00:00:22.397 --> 00:00:23.980
But what I'm going
to talk about today

00:00:23.980 --> 00:00:27.610
is the contributions
of many people

00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:29.860
that make all of
this work possible

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:32.259
and drive the energy
and enthusiasm

00:00:32.259 --> 00:00:35.150
of this project and so
much of what we talk about.

00:00:35.150 --> 00:00:38.740
I mean, so just in
this room contributing

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:40.660
to what I'm going
to talk about today,

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:44.170
we have [INAUDIBLE]
and Jenny and two

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:47.260
of our staff here, Kerry
Jordan and Francois Michonneau.

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:50.020
We have Greg Wilson, the
founder of Software Carpentry,

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:52.194
Jason Williams, who served
on the executive council

00:00:52.194 --> 00:00:53.860
of Software Carpentry,
Kate Hertweck who

00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:55.651
is on the current
executive council, Arthur

00:00:55.651 --> 00:00:58.450
Graham on the Data
Carpentry executive council.

00:00:58.450 --> 00:01:01.810
So this is really
a community effort.

00:01:01.810 --> 00:01:06.490
And so I want to attribute that
and represent that in this talk

00:01:06.490 --> 00:01:08.770
because it's a part of
that inclusive approach

00:01:08.770 --> 00:01:11.230
that we're trying to take.

00:01:11.230 --> 00:01:14.350
So I think we've seen throughout
this meeting something that's

00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:18.100
really important is that we now
have data and tools to advance

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:21.850
progress and address questions
in science and society.

00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:24.040
There's so much potential
in what everybody's

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:26.890
been talking about in the last
couple of days with the data.

00:01:26.890 --> 00:01:28.660
And then just, I
mean, the revolution

00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:31.810
in the tools available
in the R ecosystem

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:34.330
has been amazing over
the last couple of years.

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:36.670
We're in a place
where we weren't even

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:39.400
a couple of years ago.

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:42.440
And we still talk a
little bit, though,

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:44.440
about the compute and the data.

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:48.520
How do we bring compute to
data or data to compute?

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:51.550
But we need to talk about
how we scale our impact,

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:55.850
talking about bringing
people to data.

00:01:55.850 --> 00:01:57.800
So you're in this room.

00:01:57.800 --> 00:01:59.780
You could be at any other talk.

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:02.660
You could be doing anything
else with your life.

00:02:02.660 --> 00:02:06.680
Why is this the most important
thing to be talking about?

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:10.070
Why is teaching people
how to work with data--

00:02:10.070 --> 00:02:11.570
we have a lot of
social challenges--

00:02:11.570 --> 00:02:14.000
why is this one
the most important?

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:16.490
And so I want to
point to this quote

00:02:16.490 --> 00:02:20.090
from Yeshimabeit Milner,
who is the founder of Data

00:02:20.090 --> 00:02:21.980
for Black Lives.

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:25.370
And that "data becomes a tool
of profound social change

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:27.920
or a weapon of political
warfare depending

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:30.320
on whose hands it is in."

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:32.600
So we need to think
about as we have

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:35.140
this data that
this data is power

00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:39.020
and that we need to democratize
these skills to give people

00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:42.170
the power of thinking
about where they're headed,

00:02:42.170 --> 00:02:44.540
of how to interpret
data, how to use

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:47.570
data to answer the questions
that are important for them.

00:02:47.570 --> 00:02:51.170
So this to me is one of the
most important questions

00:02:51.170 --> 00:02:54.560
that we're facing right now as
we look ahead to where we're

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:59.360
going and seeing a data-driven
world ahead of us, in fact,

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:00.320
that we're in already.

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:06.900
So then if this is the most
important question, getting

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:09.390
more people to be able
to work with data,

00:03:09.390 --> 00:03:13.830
how do we scale the number
of people who can do this?

00:03:13.830 --> 00:03:16.490
So that's what we're trying to
think about in the Carpentry.

00:03:16.490 --> 00:03:19.350
So The Carpentries is a
nonprofit organization.

00:03:19.350 --> 00:03:22.080
And we're an open, global
community teaching researchers

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:25.300
the skills to turn
data into knowledge.

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:27.270
And we do this
through curriculum,

00:03:27.270 --> 00:03:31.320
open collaborative curriculum,
instructor training, teaching

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:34.230
people how to teach,
and community,

00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:36.330
by building and
fostering a community

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:41.690
around these practices and
about sharing knowledge.

00:03:41.690 --> 00:03:44.450
So I'm going to try an
analog survey approach here.

00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:46.590
We'll see how it goes.

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:51.260
So in reflecting on your
own learning experiences,

00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:53.102
who in this room
has been-- and I'm

00:03:53.102 --> 00:03:55.310
going to tell you a way to
answer this question-- who

00:03:55.310 --> 00:03:57.980
in this room has been in
a course or a workshop

00:03:57.980 --> 00:03:59.750
where they felt like
they didn't belong?

00:03:59.750 --> 00:04:02.400
So if you say, yes, to that,
we're going to clap twice.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:03.072
OK?

00:04:03.072 --> 00:04:04.550
[CLAP, CLAP]

00:04:04.550 --> 00:04:05.120
OK.

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:08.000
So that's a couple of people.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:09.820
Good analog survey approach.

00:04:09.820 --> 00:04:10.400
OK.

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:13.400
So how many people, after
having that experience,

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:15.931
left that topic for a while?

00:04:15.931 --> 00:04:18.339
[CLAP, CLAP]

00:04:18.339 --> 00:04:20.829
So we have no idea how
many people that is.

00:04:20.829 --> 00:04:22.960
But at least, it's a few, right?

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:26.500
So feeling like you
belong in that course

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:32.360
is really important for how you
continue to learn that topic.

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:34.572
So in The Carpentries,
I'll talk about how many

00:04:34.572 --> 00:04:35.530
workshops we've taught.

00:04:35.530 --> 00:04:37.290
But it's been a lot.

00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:38.980
And so something
that we realized

00:04:38.980 --> 00:04:41.350
when we were trying
to measure our impact

00:04:41.350 --> 00:04:43.600
or think about
what was important

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:46.570
is that we realized it's not
about a specific skill set.

00:04:46.570 --> 00:04:49.480
It's not about, can you
parse this line of code?

00:04:49.480 --> 00:04:52.450
Can you construct a
function in this way?

00:04:52.450 --> 00:04:55.810
But that it's about establishing
a growth mindset around data

00:04:55.810 --> 00:04:58.490
and code and people
feeling like they belong,

00:04:58.490 --> 00:05:01.940
that data science is for them.

00:05:01.940 --> 00:05:05.490
And so the growth mindset is a
bit of a loaded term right now.

00:05:05.490 --> 00:05:07.630
And does it work,
does it not work?

00:05:07.630 --> 00:05:09.490
So I'm not going
to talk about that.

00:05:09.490 --> 00:05:13.090
But what I do want to say
is, what does it mean to us

00:05:13.090 --> 00:05:15.370
right now when we say that?

00:05:15.370 --> 00:05:16.970
So it means confidence.

00:05:16.970 --> 00:05:18.700
The confidence that
this is something

00:05:18.700 --> 00:05:22.390
you can do or continue doing.

00:05:22.390 --> 00:05:25.300
That we focus on effort
and incremental learning

00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:26.357
and not outcomes.

00:05:26.357 --> 00:05:27.940
That you don't need
to immediately get

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:29.380
something right.

00:05:29.380 --> 00:05:32.560
And seeing mistakes as
opportunities for learning

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:35.020
and having an opportunity
for a safe space

00:05:35.020 --> 00:05:37.000
to make those mistakes
to continue learning.

00:05:39.980 --> 00:05:43.190
So we want to work towards
this growth mindset using

00:05:43.190 --> 00:05:44.560
inclusive pedagogy.

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:48.200
So not focusing-- we do
focus on what we teach.

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:49.190
That is a big thing.

00:05:49.190 --> 00:05:51.560
I'm not talking
about that right now.

00:05:51.560 --> 00:05:54.060
But how we teach.

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:56.052
And so that's what I
want to talk about now.

00:05:56.052 --> 00:05:58.010
And so we're thinking
about it in the context--

00:05:58.010 --> 00:05:59.900
and for the people that are
in there, and they're saying,

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:01.940
I've never actually
heard us say it this way.

00:06:01.940 --> 00:06:03.320
That is true.

00:06:03.320 --> 00:06:05.780
So we're thinking about
taking all of the things

00:06:05.780 --> 00:06:08.900
that we do every day and
stepping back and saying, what

00:06:08.900 --> 00:06:13.880
is it that this represents is
these four A's, Accessible,

00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:16.355
Approachable,
Aligned, and Active.

00:06:18.910 --> 00:06:21.540
So what are these things mean
in the context of a learning

00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:22.590
environment?

00:06:22.590 --> 00:06:26.280
Accessible, so available
in a time and a place

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:27.930
where people can attend.

00:06:27.930 --> 00:06:31.320
So that's really important
because if you have things

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:35.280
on weekends and you can't
have child care on weekends,

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:36.390
you can't go on weekends.

00:06:36.390 --> 00:06:39.720
But maybe you can't get away
from work during the week.

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:41.970
Or maybe only can do evenings.

00:06:41.970 --> 00:06:43.770
Maybe you only can
do online because you

00:06:43.770 --> 00:06:45.000
can't leave your house.

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:48.720
There are a lot of different
ways that we can deliver things

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:51.210
so that it's accessible
to people in a time

00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:54.200
and a place that works for them.

00:06:54.200 --> 00:06:57.300
That it's welcoming so
that when they are there

00:06:57.300 --> 00:07:00.810
that it is accessible to them
because of the environment

00:07:00.810 --> 00:07:01.810
that's created.

00:07:01.810 --> 00:07:04.470
So in all of our workshops,
we have a code of conduct.

00:07:04.470 --> 00:07:06.670
And we have a
reporting structure.

00:07:06.670 --> 00:07:10.770
Those are both important, not
just having a code of conduct.

00:07:10.770 --> 00:07:11.970
And we start with that.

00:07:11.970 --> 00:07:13.830
I appreciate in this
meeting that they

00:07:13.830 --> 00:07:17.460
started with a code of conduct
because it sets the stage

00:07:17.460 --> 00:07:19.110
as saying that this
is a place that

00:07:19.110 --> 00:07:23.820
is supposed to be accessible
and welcoming to everybody.

00:07:23.820 --> 00:07:27.900
And being proactive about
ensuring accessibility needs.

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:31.590
So I think we still have
work to do in this front.

00:07:31.590 --> 00:07:34.050
But looking at your
website resources,

00:07:34.050 --> 00:07:36.240
looking at your color schemes.

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:38.310
A lot of great R
packages around that.

00:07:38.310 --> 00:07:42.850
Looking through the lens of
accessibility for everybody,

00:07:42.850 --> 00:07:45.225
not just for the largest
percentage of learners.

00:07:48.730 --> 00:07:50.020
Approachable.

00:07:50.020 --> 00:07:52.300
So this is accessible.

00:07:52.300 --> 00:07:53.560
I can go to this thing.

00:07:53.560 --> 00:07:55.300
But do I want to go?

00:07:55.300 --> 00:07:56.860
Is this for me?

00:07:56.860 --> 00:07:58.660
And so that starts
at the very beginning

00:07:58.660 --> 00:08:01.180
and advertising and
recruitment for the workshop.

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:03.880
If this is for
beginners, say, this

00:08:03.880 --> 00:08:07.990
is for people with little
to no prior experience

00:08:07.990 --> 00:08:10.750
in this domain, map
out, essentially,

00:08:10.750 --> 00:08:14.050
what your persona is of this
person who can come and tell

00:08:14.050 --> 00:08:17.380
these people who it's for
and why they're welcome.

00:08:17.380 --> 00:08:20.770
Invite them to participate.

00:08:20.770 --> 00:08:23.320
Meeting learners where they are.

00:08:23.320 --> 00:08:27.160
So understanding that someone
might be working in Excel

00:08:27.160 --> 00:08:30.820
and that they maybe
primarily only

00:08:30.820 --> 00:08:33.580
had experience with their
computers for Netflix.

00:08:33.580 --> 00:08:34.840
That's not a bad thing.

00:08:34.840 --> 00:08:36.460
That's where people are.

00:08:36.460 --> 00:08:39.549
Let's appreciate where people
are and meet them there

00:08:39.549 --> 00:08:42.220
rather than hoping that
they're someplace else.

00:08:42.220 --> 00:08:44.290
And that is what helps
make it approachable.

00:08:44.290 --> 00:08:45.850
We're creating that
on-ramp for them.

00:08:45.850 --> 00:08:48.060
And we're putting the
on-ramp in front of them

00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:49.960
instead of like way
the heck over there

00:08:49.960 --> 00:08:52.810
or a humongous step in
front of them to start.

00:08:52.810 --> 00:08:55.150
And that helps overcome
activation barriers

00:08:55.150 --> 00:08:58.240
to getting started.

00:08:58.240 --> 00:09:00.980
And then, of course, the
mistakes are the pedagogy.

00:09:00.980 --> 00:09:02.830
So in our workshops,
so we'll talk

00:09:02.830 --> 00:09:07.330
about how we use like a
participatory live coding

00:09:07.330 --> 00:09:08.020
approach.

00:09:08.020 --> 00:09:11.200
So you always make mistakes
when you're doing it in person.

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:13.790
But we treat that as a part
of the learning opportunity.

00:09:13.790 --> 00:09:16.480
Seeing other people,
when they make mistakes,

00:09:16.480 --> 00:09:18.970
when they don't know
what's going on,

00:09:18.970 --> 00:09:23.140
helps you understand, one, how
you approach fixing a mistake,

00:09:23.140 --> 00:09:24.430
and also that that's OK.

00:09:24.430 --> 00:09:27.220
And I think since we're
supposed to quote Hadley, that's

00:09:27.220 --> 00:09:29.970
something that
Hadley always says is

00:09:29.970 --> 00:09:32.380
that he has to Google things.

00:09:32.380 --> 00:09:34.790
It's not that everyone knows
everything and never makes

00:09:34.790 --> 00:09:35.290
mistakes.

00:09:38.470 --> 00:09:40.920
Aligned.

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:42.350
They're all sort of similar.

00:09:42.350 --> 00:09:45.510
But the aligned is really
about the motivation.

00:09:45.510 --> 00:09:48.670
So teaching with data sets
that are relevant to the user.

00:09:48.670 --> 00:09:51.120
So we had a great discussion
this morning at the Data

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:54.100
For Good [INAUDIBLE]
about using data

00:09:54.100 --> 00:09:57.510
sets that the students
were really excited about.

00:09:57.510 --> 00:09:59.860
And that got them
excited about the topic.

00:09:59.860 --> 00:10:01.860
Because if you're trying
to introduce some topic

00:10:01.860 --> 00:10:05.010
that they're not excited
about and teach a skill on top

00:10:05.010 --> 00:10:07.450
of that, you're trying to do
two things at the same time.

00:10:07.450 --> 00:10:10.920
So starting with a topic that
people are excited about.

00:10:10.920 --> 00:10:12.900
Teaching the skills
and perspectives

00:10:12.900 --> 00:10:14.700
relevant to their current work.

00:10:14.700 --> 00:10:15.420
So, yeah.

00:10:15.420 --> 00:10:18.660
You might hold down the road
think that everybody should

00:10:18.660 --> 00:10:19.860
know C++.

00:10:19.860 --> 00:10:21.840
But if that person is
not going to use it

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:23.970
right away in
their context, then

00:10:23.970 --> 00:10:26.310
it's not going to be
something that sticks,

00:10:26.310 --> 00:10:29.310
that understands-- they
understand why it's important

00:10:29.310 --> 00:10:33.090
and will motivate them
to keep using the tool.

00:10:33.090 --> 00:10:36.750
Instructors that are current in
the field, so peer instruction.

00:10:36.750 --> 00:10:38.730
So some of our best
instructors are

00:10:38.730 --> 00:10:42.660
people who were learners only
a year ago because, one, they

00:10:42.660 --> 00:10:44.280
understand the misconceptions.

00:10:44.280 --> 00:10:46.666
They're very familiar with
what that process looks like.

00:10:46.666 --> 00:10:48.540
So when they see that
something doesn't-- one

00:10:48.540 --> 00:10:52.350
doesn't understand something,
then they can relate to that,

00:10:52.350 --> 00:10:54.690
help guide someone
through that process.

00:10:54.690 --> 00:10:55.890
They also are peers.

00:10:55.890 --> 00:11:00.180
They're an ecologist or a social
scientist or an economist.

00:11:00.180 --> 00:11:02.730
And so they can put the
things into context.

00:11:02.730 --> 00:11:05.520
And so it helps in guiding
their learning as well.

00:11:05.520 --> 00:11:07.890
And teaching the current
tools, so looking around.

00:11:07.890 --> 00:11:09.450
What is it that
people are using?

00:11:09.450 --> 00:11:11.850
Teach what you're
using in your work.

00:11:11.850 --> 00:11:14.310
And again, that's going to
be something that helps them

00:11:14.310 --> 00:11:17.080
continue with their learning.

00:11:17.080 --> 00:11:18.530
And finally, active.

00:11:18.530 --> 00:11:21.870
And this is one of the ones that
we really like to talk about.

00:11:21.870 --> 00:11:26.700
And this is a little bit
that idea of guided practice,

00:11:26.700 --> 00:11:28.380
that you can get
feedback on what you're

00:11:28.380 --> 00:11:29.730
doing as you're learning.

00:11:29.730 --> 00:11:32.340
That's one of the challenges
of learning on your own

00:11:32.340 --> 00:11:36.300
is that you often can't get
that feedback right away.

00:11:36.300 --> 00:11:38.814
And so participatory
live coding,

00:11:38.814 --> 00:11:40.230
that's the instructor
at the front

00:11:40.230 --> 00:11:44.550
of the room going through code
and having the learners go

00:11:44.550 --> 00:11:46.390
through it at the same time.

00:11:46.390 --> 00:11:50.160
So you're participating
together in doing that coding.

00:11:50.160 --> 00:11:52.390
An I-We-You model for teaching.

00:11:52.390 --> 00:11:53.850
So I describe something.

00:11:53.850 --> 00:11:54.900
We do it together.

00:11:54.900 --> 00:11:57.090
You do it yourself.

00:11:57.090 --> 00:11:59.350
It gives you a chance to
try it out on your own

00:11:59.350 --> 00:12:03.200
but not completely
without a safety net.

00:12:03.200 --> 00:12:06.120
All of our curriculum is
open-source and collaboratively

00:12:06.120 --> 00:12:08.340
developed and
updated to represent

00:12:08.340 --> 00:12:09.360
the current practices.

00:12:09.360 --> 00:12:13.320
So our curriculum is active,
actually, in and of itself.

00:12:13.320 --> 00:12:14.040
Oh, I see.

00:12:14.040 --> 00:12:16.560
[INAUDIBLE],, in fact,
introduced Tidyverse

00:12:16.560 --> 00:12:18.690
to our Data Carpentry lessons.

00:12:18.690 --> 00:12:21.750
So we have people who
are in the field that

00:12:21.750 --> 00:12:23.380
are active and
engaged in the topics

00:12:23.380 --> 00:12:26.970
so that they kind of meet
those first three As.

00:12:26.970 --> 00:12:28.670
We have helpers
in our workshops.

00:12:28.670 --> 00:12:30.870
So not only the instructor
at the front of the room

00:12:30.870 --> 00:12:32.490
but someone who goes
around and talks

00:12:32.490 --> 00:12:37.050
with people to create a
strong 5 to 1 student ratio.

00:12:37.050 --> 00:12:39.180
And then, finally,
our most famous

00:12:39.180 --> 00:12:43.980
probably is formative assessment
through using sticky notes.

00:12:43.980 --> 00:12:46.890
So you completed an exercise.

00:12:46.890 --> 00:12:49.250
You put a green sticky
note on your laptop.

00:12:49.250 --> 00:12:52.050
And if you're having trouble,
you put a pink sticky note.

00:12:52.050 --> 00:12:54.090
And someone will come help you.

00:12:54.090 --> 00:12:55.800
So it's a way
during the workshop,

00:12:55.800 --> 00:12:58.050
not just waiting till the
end and saying, oh, my gosh.

00:12:58.050 --> 00:13:00.900
If people get it, here's a
survey of seeing as you go,

00:13:00.900 --> 00:13:03.180
getting a sense of the
room, assigning help

00:13:03.180 --> 00:13:04.030
when it's needed.

00:13:04.030 --> 00:13:06.120
So it's a form of
formative assessment,

00:13:06.120 --> 00:13:08.340
assessing as you're going
during the workshop.

00:13:11.200 --> 00:13:13.800
So there was a bunch--
like our whole concepts

00:13:13.800 --> 00:13:17.470
of instructor classroom training
that is two days, smushed

00:13:17.470 --> 00:13:19.850
into about five minutes.

00:13:19.850 --> 00:13:21.730
So you saw some of the
instructor training

00:13:21.730 --> 00:13:23.620
earlier this week
if you were there.

00:13:23.620 --> 00:13:25.570
But all of our instructor
training curriculum

00:13:25.570 --> 00:13:27.670
is also online.

00:13:27.670 --> 00:13:30.850
Like all of our lessons,
it's all [INAUDIBLE]..

00:13:30.850 --> 00:13:34.510
So the question is, if
what we're aiming for

00:13:34.510 --> 00:13:40.540
is confidence and continued
learning, how are we doing?

00:13:40.540 --> 00:13:43.660
How does this approach
work in terms of meeting

00:13:43.660 --> 00:13:46.237
those identified goals?

00:13:46.237 --> 00:13:48.070
So first, how many
workshops have we taught?

00:13:48.070 --> 00:13:52.300
So since 2012, we have
over 1,600 instructors,

00:13:52.300 --> 00:14:00.060
over 39,000 learners, and we've
taught over 1,700 workshops.

00:14:00.060 --> 00:14:03.260
So not only have we
taught a lot of them,

00:14:03.260 --> 00:14:04.680
we've had a global reach.

00:14:04.680 --> 00:14:07.620
So this is a map of the
workshops over time,

00:14:07.620 --> 00:14:10.980
getting brighter in color
as we have more workshops.

00:14:10.980 --> 00:14:12.480
And Francois did
this great plot.

00:14:12.480 --> 00:14:15.810
And I'm super-excited
about it so [LAUGHS]..

00:14:15.810 --> 00:14:18.390
And you can see we got
Antarctica in last year.

00:14:18.390 --> 00:14:21.000
So, very excited about that.

00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:22.050
One person taught that.

00:14:22.050 --> 00:14:24.720
We think we should just
put her name on Antarctica.

00:14:24.720 --> 00:14:27.090
She just claims the whole thing.

00:14:27.090 --> 00:14:29.310
So we've also been
able to have--

00:14:29.310 --> 00:14:32.790
because of this volunteer
approach, approaching teaching

00:14:32.790 --> 00:14:36.210
from the perspective of
meeting learners where they are

00:14:36.210 --> 00:14:38.550
and scaling impact,
we've been able to reach

00:14:38.550 --> 00:14:40.222
a lot of different communities.

00:14:43.320 --> 00:14:44.310
OK.

00:14:44.310 --> 00:14:46.740
So the first one we're trying
to achieve is confidence.

00:14:46.740 --> 00:14:48.150
So how did we do?

00:14:48.150 --> 00:14:50.130
So this is a survey of students.

00:14:50.130 --> 00:14:51.960
This is just Data Carpentry.

00:14:51.960 --> 00:14:55.110
It's not all R. Most of
Data Carpentries are R.

00:14:55.110 --> 00:14:57.360
But some are Python as well.

00:14:57.360 --> 00:15:00.960
So pre and post,
two days, how much

00:15:00.960 --> 00:15:05.490
has people's perception of their
abilities and their confidence

00:15:05.490 --> 00:15:06.690
changed?

00:15:06.690 --> 00:15:09.270
So the first question is, I'm
able to write a program that

00:15:09.270 --> 00:15:12.330
would solve a small problem.

00:15:12.330 --> 00:15:16.560
So we see these full point
differences around programs.

00:15:16.560 --> 00:15:18.880
I'm able to search for answers.

00:15:18.880 --> 00:15:22.530
I'm able to make my
programming more reproducible,

00:15:22.530 --> 00:15:26.010
overcome a problem, become
more efficient in programming.

00:15:26.010 --> 00:15:28.080
I know to keep my raw data raw.

00:15:28.080 --> 00:15:31.230
I'm very invested
in that question.

00:15:31.230 --> 00:15:32.790
And then that last
one, confidence

00:15:32.790 --> 00:15:35.430
in programming, so I have
confidence in my ability

00:15:35.430 --> 00:15:36.600
to program.

00:15:36.600 --> 00:15:38.067
That was one of
our lowest scores

00:15:38.067 --> 00:15:39.150
right before the workshop.

00:15:39.150 --> 00:15:42.390
And it's increasing a
full point in two days.

00:15:42.390 --> 00:15:44.520
I mean, you might say
maybe that confidence

00:15:44.520 --> 00:15:47.220
is misplaced after two days.

00:15:47.220 --> 00:15:50.520
But the point is is that
they feel like it's for them.

00:15:50.520 --> 00:15:52.924
People feel like it's for them.

00:15:52.924 --> 00:15:54.840
And so these are some
of the words that people

00:15:54.840 --> 00:15:56.610
use to describe
the workshop that

00:15:56.610 --> 00:16:00.960
reflects those ideals that
we have around inclusivity

00:16:00.960 --> 00:16:02.700
and accessibility.

00:16:02.700 --> 00:16:05.710
And you always see
instructors and helpful.

00:16:05.710 --> 00:16:07.950
And so it represents
a lot of those things

00:16:07.950 --> 00:16:11.370
that I've talked about
with our practices.

00:16:11.370 --> 00:16:15.150
Not only are people more
confident after two days,

00:16:15.150 --> 00:16:17.370
but when we survey people
more than six months

00:16:17.370 --> 00:16:20.670
after a workshop-- and this
does have a bias because only

00:16:20.670 --> 00:16:22.970
certain people answer
surveys that you send out

00:16:22.970 --> 00:16:26.130
way along later.

00:16:26.130 --> 00:16:27.660
What is the percentage?

00:16:27.660 --> 00:16:32.070
Basically 100-- almost 100%
of people more than six months

00:16:32.070 --> 00:16:35.939
after a workshop say that
they are more confident.

00:16:35.939 --> 00:16:36.480
I don't know.

00:16:36.480 --> 00:16:37.521
You don't look surprised.

00:16:37.521 --> 00:16:39.270
I'm surprised [LAUGHS].

00:16:39.270 --> 00:16:40.314
I do programming.

00:16:40.314 --> 00:16:41.730
I don't always
feel more confident

00:16:41.730 --> 00:16:44.850
after I've spent a day
programming, I can tell you.

00:16:44.850 --> 00:16:47.340
So I mean, this to
me that is not only

00:16:47.340 --> 00:16:49.530
this sort of short-term
effect of two days

00:16:49.530 --> 00:16:54.330
but that long-term, this feeling
persists is very important.

00:16:54.330 --> 00:16:55.140
Continued learning.

00:16:55.140 --> 00:16:57.870
Do people go on to
continue learning?

00:16:57.870 --> 00:17:01.650
So this is, again, that
longer term survey.

00:17:01.650 --> 00:17:04.109
Have you gone on to continue
learning after the workshop?

00:17:04.109 --> 00:17:06.300
So most people agree or
strongly agree that they've

00:17:06.300 --> 00:17:09.210
gone on to continue learning.

00:17:09.210 --> 00:17:12.180
And these are using these
skills in their work,

00:17:12.180 --> 00:17:15.569
improving overall
efficiency, managing data.

00:17:15.569 --> 00:17:18.869
So people are regularly
using these practices.

00:17:18.869 --> 00:17:21.000
Again, when they
come back to reflect

00:17:21.000 --> 00:17:23.670
on what this has meant to them,
what they're capable of now,

00:17:23.670 --> 00:17:27.359
these are some of the
words that they use.

00:17:27.359 --> 00:17:30.690
And they strongly recommend
their workshops to others.

00:17:30.690 --> 00:17:36.010
So almost 80% have recommended
the workshop to someone else.

00:17:36.010 --> 00:17:37.560
Our Net Promoter score--

00:17:37.560 --> 00:17:40.250
if you're into Net Promoter
scores-- is like over 9.

00:17:40.250 --> 00:17:42.300
Is that the right thing?

00:17:42.300 --> 00:17:44.580
The number of people that
want to recommend these

00:17:44.580 --> 00:17:47.040
to other people
also reflects on how

00:17:47.040 --> 00:17:50.010
they felt about that
experience, not just

00:17:50.010 --> 00:17:53.990
about what they
thought they learned.

00:17:53.990 --> 00:17:55.709
So safe spaces for
continued learning,

00:17:55.709 --> 00:17:57.500
that was that last one
around a [INAUDIBLE]

00:17:57.500 --> 00:17:59.780
mindset because we want
people to continue learning.

00:17:59.780 --> 00:18:01.650
How do they do that?

00:18:01.650 --> 00:18:04.280
So one way that they're seeing
that, people will continue

00:18:04.280 --> 00:18:06.020
to engage with our
community, sometimes

00:18:06.020 --> 00:18:08.060
going on to become
instructors or helpers.

00:18:10.784 --> 00:18:12.200
But they do a lot
of other things.

00:18:12.200 --> 00:18:14.030
Sometimes they use
Carpentry lessons.

00:18:14.030 --> 00:18:15.110
They use short courses.

00:18:15.110 --> 00:18:15.950
They go to meetups.

00:18:15.950 --> 00:18:18.590
They go to
semester-long courses.

00:18:18.590 --> 00:18:20.735
So when we talk about
this after the workshop,

00:18:20.735 --> 00:18:21.610
we know some of that.

00:18:21.610 --> 00:18:23.568
But what they're really
doing is they're diving

00:18:23.568 --> 00:18:24.920
into a community of practice.

00:18:24.920 --> 00:18:28.830
And so seeing these practices
that exist is really important.

00:18:28.830 --> 00:18:31.940
And so we're lucky here with
our ladies and all the things

00:18:31.940 --> 00:18:33.620
that people talked
about that they

00:18:33.620 --> 00:18:35.690
do have welcoming
communities of practice

00:18:35.690 --> 00:18:36.980
to continue to learn in.

00:18:39.557 --> 00:18:41.140
So see that there's
some elements that

00:18:41.140 --> 00:18:43.510
make this important.

00:18:43.510 --> 00:18:46.200
And so then the
question is, so great.

00:18:46.200 --> 00:18:47.020
Awesome.

00:18:47.020 --> 00:18:47.859
People like it.

00:18:47.859 --> 00:18:49.900
But I said at the beginning
that one of our goals

00:18:49.900 --> 00:18:51.370
is reaching diverse communities.

00:18:51.370 --> 00:18:53.040
And how are we doing?

00:18:53.040 --> 00:18:56.050
Well, gender, I guess would
say pretty rep-- this is just

00:18:56.050 --> 00:18:57.850
in the United States
because that's

00:18:57.850 --> 00:19:00.100
the only place we collect
demographic data because

00:19:00.100 --> 00:19:02.200
of legal reasons.

00:19:02.200 --> 00:19:04.700
We're not doing so well.

00:19:04.700 --> 00:19:07.220
So we need to do better.

00:19:07.220 --> 00:19:09.700
So there are elements of
creating a space where

00:19:09.700 --> 00:19:11.440
people feel welcome.

00:19:11.440 --> 00:19:12.940
There's more
opportunities for that.

00:19:12.940 --> 00:19:16.090
There's more opportunities
for recruitment.

00:19:16.090 --> 00:19:20.076
And so what we want to say here
is that with this ecosystem,

00:19:20.076 --> 00:19:22.450
with this community of practice
that people are building,

00:19:22.450 --> 00:19:24.408
with all of these different
teaching practices,

00:19:24.408 --> 00:19:26.620
that we together
are an ecosystem.

00:19:26.620 --> 00:19:30.314
And that if we want to
go fast, we can go alone.

00:19:30.314 --> 00:19:32.230
And then if we want to
go far, we go together.

00:19:32.230 --> 00:19:34.690
And there's great
opportunities for that here.

00:19:34.690 --> 00:19:37.300
And we look forward
to continuing

00:19:37.300 --> 00:19:39.550
to work with all of
these communities

00:19:39.550 --> 00:19:42.820
and building our future
together around data.

00:19:42.820 --> 00:19:45.460
Thank you.

00:19:45.460 --> 00:19:47.910
[BLING SOUND]

